+## Correction (11-02, after trial t1): the stock rows' gap to upstream was mostly driver pacing
+
+- The "~6% slower" above is not an execution cost of the port. t1 (8f0fd2a, every block AB1ng) / tu0: ratio of sums 1.064
+  on general blocks, median of per-block ratios 0.999; by block number mod 16, residue 12 carries +845 ms (ratio 1.709),
+  the other residues lie in 0.977-1.006.
+- Residue 12 is the block after reth starts a 16-block persistence write (after the commit of the block at residue 11).
+  The write takes the same time in both runs (median 2.35 s t1, 2.38 s tu0), but the driver sends the next block later
+  with upstream (median 2.65 s against 2.17 s after that commit), so the block at residue 12 arrives while the write is
+  still running in 33 of 38 cases for t1 and 8 of 38 for tu0. The 25 blocks that overlap the write in t1 only: 1307 ms
+  against 544 ms.
+- Why the driver is slower with upstream: run_xl_f.py's srtx check sleeps 0.3 s when the block's `eap sr` line is absent
+  and is marked done only once a line is seen; the helper nice check sleeps 0.2 s for configurations with pwnbval=. The
+  upstream binary writes neither line, so in tu0 both waits happen on every block (driver time between blocks without
+  the settle: 0.45 s on AB1ng-labelled blocks, 0.66 s on AB5ng-labelled ones; 0.15 s in t1 and tq0).
+- Without residue-12 blocks, against tu0 (general / all): t1 AB1ng 0.994 / 0.995, tq0 AB1ng 0.984 / 0.981, tq0 AB5ng
+  0.700 / 0.647. Configurations rotated within one run share the pacing (tq0: 0.151 s and 0.152 s), so in-run pairings
+  are not affected. scripts/x290_stockcmp.py and scripts/x290_persist.py (engine-access-plan 54d21185).
+- What remains: the engine thread group's CPU time is 13% higher in t1 than in tu0 (0.401 / 0.350 ms per M gas) while
