diff --git a/crates/engine/tree/src/tree/eap.rs b/crates/engine/tree/src/tree/eap.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..4a705b50509d55a8f59891a62bf5ae00b22e985c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/engine/tree/src/tree/eap.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,12041 @@
+//! EngineAccessPlan research prototype (not upstream reth): storage prefetch driven by per-code-hash
+//! access plans on the engine's block execution path ("Prologue"; X-027 port of reth 2.5.1 6388aae to
+//! reth 2.9.0 and evm2).
+//!
+//! Access plans (`frame_plan`) map a call's input, caller, value and block environment to the
+//! storage slots the call is predicted to read. While a block executes, predicted slots are read by
+//! reader threads through a cache-filling provider over the parent state, the same way prewarming
+//! fills the shared execution cache, so the execution thread finds them as cache hits:
+//!
+//! - transaction arrival (`tx`): at block start, reader threads instantiate the plan of each
+//!   transaction's target with the transaction's own fields, and read sender, target and (`code`)
+//!   the target's code;
+//! - call start on the prewarm threads (`pwcs`): the prewarm inspector ([`EapPwInspector`])
+//!   instantiates the callee's plan with the frame's real input, caller and value and hands the
+//!   keys to the readers; the speculative pass ([`NbInspector`], [`NbDb`]) hands the slots it
+//!   cannot read from the cache to the readers.
+//!
+//! Reader threads are stopped and joined before `execute_block` returns (or, with `rdjoinlate`, at
+//! the next block's start, behind the cache generation guard).
+//!
+//! The configuration is read per block from the file named by `EAP_CTRL` (`key=value` tokens, see
+//! [`Mode`]). Without `EAP_CTRL` nothing changes. Plans are loaded once, in the background, from
+//! `EAP_PLANS` (`<code hash hex>.eapf`). One line per block goes to the log target `reth::eap`.
+//!
+//! X-027: not ported (off in the final configuration): calldata addresses, page prefetch, batch
+//! splitting, BN254/GLV, lock-free write tables, the mempool stage, the execution-thread inspector,
+//! storage discovery and fresh-state runs, state-root hints and prefetch, transaction chains. Their
+//! `Mode` fields and switch parses are kept, so the lines that echo switches print the same values.
+
